                <pb facs="0699" n="571" sameAs="frus:frus1885/pg571"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">No. 416.</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Morgan to Mr. Frelinghuysen</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1885-01-01"><hi rendition="#italic">January</hi> 1, 1885</date>.
                        (Received January 9.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 960.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Yesterday Mr. Thomas R. Monahan called at
                    the legation. He informed me that a few days ago the judge of the federal court
                    at Toluca sent for him and informed him that he had been “honorably acquitted,”
                    and was therefore discharged. He also stated that the judge had exacted a
                    promise from him to appear in case the supreme court should reverse the
                    judgment. He was not tried but simply discharged, he says.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>P. H. MORGAN.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
